<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
<overview>
<p>
A derivation directly overrides one of <code>configurePhase</code>,
<code>buildPhase</code>, <code>checkPhase</code>, or
<code>installPhase</code>. Replacing an entire stdenv phase bypasses the
default implementation and any setup-hook behavior layered onto it.
</p>
<p>
In nixpkgs, the supported extension points are usually the corresponding
<code>preX</code> and <code>postX</code> hooks (for example,
<code>preBuild</code> / <code>postBuild</code>), plus the phase-specific
flags and helper attributes exposed by the generic builder.
</p>
<p>
This query intentionally reports the presence of the explicit override
itself, even if the replacement body manually calls
<code>runHook preX</code> / <code>runHook postX</code>. Missing hook calls are
covered by a separate rule.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Prefer the corresponding <code>preX</code> / <code>postX</code> hooks and the
builder's existing flags over fully replacing the phase. Only override the
whole phase when the default implementation is genuinely unsuitable.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>Problematic:</p>
<sample language="nix">
{ stdenv }:
stdenv.mkDerivation {
  pname = "demo";
  version = "1.0";

  buildPhase = ''
    make all
  '';
}
</sample>
<p>Preferred:</p>
<sample language="nix">
{ stdenv }:
stdenv.mkDerivation {
  pname = "demo";
  version = "1.0";

  preBuild = ''
    echo "preparing build"
  '';

  postBuild = ''
    echo "build finished"
  '';
}
</sample>
</example>

<references>
<li>
<a href="https://github.com/jtojnar/nixpkgs-hammering/blob/main/explanations/explicit-phases.md">nixpkgs-hammering: explicit-phases</a>
</li>
<li>
Nixpkgs Manual:
<a href="https://nixos.org/manual/nixpkgs/unstable/#ssec-controlling-phases">Controlling phases</a>
</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
